--
--
Documents the current state of [temp.deduct.funcaddr]: template
argument deduction from the target type of a function-address
context.

CBMC's implementation (added in 18e846497c) handles the std::endl
case where the function parameter is a class-template instantiation
and matching instances can be found in the symbol table.  It does
NOT yet handle the simpler case where the target type is a plain
function pointer with scalar parameter types, as in
  apply(&id_sum, 3, 4)
with
  template <class T> int id_sum(T a, T b);
  int apply(int (*)(int, int), int, int);

After the 2026 "template-only no-match silently throws" fix, the
surface symptom changed: instead of emitting 'CONVERSION ERROR' for
the unresolved 'id_sum' template, CBMC now silently drops the
containing main() body (convert_function catches the throw and
discards the partially-elaborated body).  Verification "succeeds"
vacuously because the surviving main is empty — the two
__CPROVER_assert statements that would have tested r==7 and r2==11
never make it into the goto program.

A real fix would thread the target-type context ([temp.deduct.funcaddr])
through the argument pre-typecheck loop in
typecheck_side_effect_function_call so that '&id_sum' in an argument
position bound to 'int (*)(int, int)' deduces T=int.
